export const RULES_TITLE = "Code of Conduct and Competition Rules";
export const RULES_VERSION = "1.0";
export const RULES_EFFECTIVE_DATE = "21 November 2026";

export const RULES_MARKDOWN =
    "# Code of Conduct and Competition Rules\n\nThis document sets out the rules, expectations, and terms governing participation in nethack. It explains what participants must provide, how projects will be evaluated, and the standards of conduct expected throughout the competition.\n\n**Version 1.0.** This version took effect on 21 November 2026 and is the binding document for the current season. Organizers may publish a later version with reasonable notice; the latest published version governs participation and submissions. Participants are responsible for reviewing it before submitting their work.\n\n**Please read before participating.**\n\nThe requirements in this document are binding. A project that works on its creator's computer is not necessarily ready for evaluation. Participants must also meet the source code, documentation, accessibility, demonstration, and conduct requirements described below.\n\n### Quick Guide: What You Need to Know Recommended for readers who prefer a simpler, plain-language explanation.\n\nThis section explains the most important rules in plain language. It is a starting point, not a replacement for reading the complete requirements below.\n\n**1. Make Your Source Code Public**\n\nYour complete project source code must be available in a public repository on a platform such as GitHub, GitLab, Codeberg, or Gitee. **Uploading only an executable file or a compressed archive is not enough.** Other people must be able to browse and download your source code.\n\n**2. Include a Complete README**\n\nYour repository must contain a file named `README.md` , written in clear, professional American English. It must explain what your project does, how to use it, which technologies you used, and where to watch your demonstration video.\n\n**3. Make Your Project Available for Testing**\n\nJudges must have a practical way to try your project. Depending on what you build:\n\n- Websites must be deployed and publicly accessible.\n\n- Desktop applications must have downloadable executables for at least one supported platform.\n\n- Games must be playable in a browser or available as a download.\n\n- Mobile applications must be available through an app store or as an APK or IPA installer.\n\nJudges should not have to create an account on another website just to see your work. If your own application requires user accounts, additional requirements and penalties may apply.\n\n**4. Follow the Demonstration Video Rules**\n\nEvery project must have a demonstration video **between two and three minutes long** . The video must contain **absolutely no audio** and must not reveal your identity, including your face, name, or voice.\n\nThe video must use H.264, AV1, or VP9 encoding and must be hosted on YouTube, Bilibili, or OneDrive/SharePoint through an accessible sharing link.\n\n**Do not upload the video to GitHub or any other Git-based hosting platform.** Doing so will result in the submission being treated as having no valid video and will lead to disqualification.\n\nIf your project includes a downloadable executable, you must also display its SHA-256 checksum in the video.\n\n**5. Make Evaluation Easy**\n\nJudges should be able to experience your project without purchasing hardware, installing uncommon software, or signing up for unrelated services. Any unavoidable access requirements must be explained in your README and may affect your evaluation.\n\n**6. Submit Your Own Work**\n\nYour project must represent meaningful original work. You may use existing libraries, frameworks, and assets when you have the right to do so and provide proper credit. Simply copying an existing project without meaningful changes is plagiarism and will result in disqualification.\n\n**7. Be Honest About AI**\n\nAI tools are allowed, but you must explain exactly which parts of your project they helped create, which tools you used, and what assistance they provided. A vague statement such as \"AI was used\" is not enough. Undeclared or misleading AI use may be treated as plagiarism.\n\n**8. Be Prepared to Explain Your Work**\n\nJudges may ask you to present your project and answer questions if they suspect excessive or undeclared AI use. All First Prize, Second Prize, Third Prize, and Honorable Mention recipients must also complete a live presentation.\n\n**9. Respect Others and Their Work**\n\nHarmful software, unauthorized access, deliberate deception, harassment, discrimination, and plagiarism are prohibited. Serious violations may be reported to the school administration.\n\n**10. Understand the Licensing Terms**\n\nBy submitting a project, you agree to the source-code licensing and public showcase terms described in this document. If you do not provide an explicit license file, the default MIT licensing provision applies as described below.\n\n**11. Understand Your Responsibilities**\n\nParticipants are responsible for their own expenses. Prize amounts are subject to confirmation, and the organizers reserve the right to amend these rules or make changes to the competition when necessary.\n\n**12. Ask Questions When Necessary**\n\nFor questions or clarification, contact **devclub-bisz@basischina.com** . Members of the BISZ Developers' Club leadership will respond.\n\n## Essential Requirements at a Glance\n\nThe following checklist summarizes the main submission requirements. Read the detailed sections for the exact rules and consequences.\n\n- **Public source code:** Complete source code must be publicly available on a recognized Git hosting platform. Executable-only submissions are not accepted.\n\n- **README:** A complete, professional `README.md` written in American English is required.\n\n- **Working demonstration:** A publicly accessible demo URL and a valid demonstration video are required.\n\n- **Evaluation access:** Judges must not be forced to create unrelated third-party accounts, purchase hardware, or install uncommon software.\n\n- **Original work:** Submissions must contain meaningful original contributions. Closed-source projects are not eligible.\n\n- **AI disclosure:** All substantial AI-generated or AI-assisted project components must be described in detail.\n\n- **Video requirements:** The video must be 2–3 minutes long, silent, and free of personal identification. It must not be hosted on a Git-based platform.\n\n- **Costs:** Participants are responsible for expenses incurred during development and participation.\n\n- **Showcase permission:** Submission grants the organizers the rights described in the public showcase license section.\n\n- **Default source-code license:** If no explicit license file is provided, the MIT License provision described below applies.\n\n**Important: Mandatory requirements**\n\nRequirements explicitly marked as mandatory must be met. Failure to meet a mandatory submission requirement will result in disqualification unless that particular rule expressly specifies a different consequence, such as a scoring penalty.\n\n## Phase 1: Plan Your Project and Understand the Rules\n\nBefore beginning development, make sure that your project fits the competition, that you have the right to use everything you include, and that you understand the responsibilities that come with submitting your work.\n\n### 1.1. Thematic Relevance\n\nEvery submission must have a meaningful connection to the competition theme. A minimal connection is required, and projects with a particularly weak or superficial connection will receive point deductions according to the judging rubric.\n\nYour project should demonstrate that you have thought about the theme and applied it to something meaningful. Simply mentioning the theme in a project description is not enough to establish a strong connection.\n\n### 1.2. Originality and Intellectual Property\n\nAll submissions must represent your team's own original work. You are welcome to build on existing technologies, including open-source libraries, frameworks, and other resources, provided that you have the legal right to use them and give appropriate credit to their creators.\n\nYou must respect all applicable licenses and intellectual property rights. Do not include copyrighted material, source code, images, music, or other assets without permission or a license that allows your intended use.\n\n**Copying or directly forking an existing project without making meaningful original contributions constitutes plagiarism.** Such submissions will be disqualified and may be reported to the school administration.\n\nUsing an existing library or framework does not, by itself, make a project unoriginal. What matters is whether your team has made meaningful contributions of its own and can explain the work it submits.\n\n### 1.3. Open-Source Development\n\nnethack is built around the belief that software development benefits from openness, transparency, and collaboration. We want participants to create work that others can study, learn from, and improve.\n\n**Closed-source submissions are not eligible.** Every project must make its complete source code publicly available under an appropriate open-source license.\n\nWe do not dismiss the value of closed-source software. Rather, this competition specifically chooses to support open development because it encourages learning, makes collaboration possible, and helps build trust within the wider software community.\n\n### 1.4. Responsible Use of Artificial Intelligence\n\n**Required disclosure.** AI tools are permitted in this competition. We recognize that they can help participants learn, explore ideas, solve problems, and develop software more efficiently. However, using AI does not remove your responsibility for the work you submit.\n\nEvery project component that was generated or substantially assisted by an AI tool must be disclosed. Your disclosure must identify:\n\n- **The specific parts involved:** Identify the components or files that received AI assistance, such as user interface components, backend logic, database structures, or scripts.\n\n- **The tools used:** Name the AI tools or models involved, such as ChatGPT, GitHub Copilot, or a locally run model.\n\n- **The type of assistance:** Explain what the tool actually did. For example, you might state that an AI tool generated a data-processing script that your team subsequently reviewed, tested, and modified.\n\nA general statement such as \"AI was used during development\" is not sufficient. The disclosure must allow judges to understand where AI contributed and how your team used its output.\n\n**Responsible and fully disclosed AI use is not considered plagiarism.** However, hiding substantial AI assistance, making misleading claims about your work, or relying on generated material that your team does not understand may result in the submission being treated as plagiarism.\n\nWe encourage participants to treat AI as a learning and development tool, not as a substitute for understanding. Being able to explain, review, test, and improve AI-assisted work is an important part of responsible software development.\n\n### 1.5. Participant Conduct and Community Standards\n\nAll participants are expected to act responsibly and treat one another with respect. Harassment, discrimination, intimidation, and other forms of harmful conduct will not be tolerated.\n\nThe organizers will assess the seriousness of reported incidents. Serious or repeated violations may be referred to the school administration for further action.\n\nParticipants are also expected to respect the work and contributions of others. Do not claim someone else's work as your own, deliberately misrepresent your contributions, or interfere with another participant's ability to take part in the competition.\n\n### 1.6. Principles of Open Participation\n\nBy participating in nethack, you agree to uphold the following principles. They form the ethical foundation of this competition and the community we hope to build.\n\n- Build with the intention of creating useful work and providing real value, not causing harm or disruption.\n\n- Share your work openly and contribute to the wider community's ability to learn and create.\n\n- Respect the same freedoms that you benefit from. Do not unnecessarily restrict others' access to knowledge, tools, or opportunities.\n\n- Act with integrity. Take responsibility for your work and respect the contributions and rights of others.\n\n## Phase 2: Build a Project That Can Be Evaluated\n\nA successful submission must be more than a collection of source files. Judges must have a practical way to access, understand, and test your project. Plan for this from the beginning rather than leaving it until the final day.\n\n### 2.1. Public Access and Demonstration\n\nEvery project must provide a publicly accessible demonstration URL. The link must lead directly to the working project, a playable version, or a page where the project can be downloaded.\n\nThe project must be available to judges without unnecessary restrictions. In particular, judges must not be required to create an account on an unrelated third-party website simply to access or experience your submission.\n\nIf authentication is an essential part of your own application, your system must allow any new user to register freely. Providing only one shared demonstration account is not acceptable. Even when registration is available, requiring judges to create an account introduces an evaluation barrier and may result in significant penalties.\n\nIf your project is hosted on a platform that inherently requires its users to sign in, you must clearly disclose this limitation in your README. You should expect such a requirement to negatively affect your evaluation.\n\n### 2.2. Requirements by Project Type\n\nThe exact form of your demonstration depends on the type of project you build. The following requirements apply:\n\n- **Websites and web applications:** Your project must be deployed on a publicly accessible server. Judges must be able to visit and use the live application through a browser. If your application requires authentication, it must allow new users to register freely. A single shared demonstration account is not sufficient.\n\n- **Desktop applications:** Your project must be compiled into a downloadable executable for at least one of the following: Windows, macOS, or Debian/Debian-based Linux. Supporting at least two of these platforms is **strongly encouraged** . The download link must lead to a page where the executable can be obtained, such as a repository release.\n\n- **Games:** Your game must be playable directly in a web browser or available as a downloadable executable. Platforms such as itch.io may be used to host browser-playable games or downloads.\n\n- **Mobile applications:** Your application must be published in the appropriate app store or made readily available through an APK installer for Android or an IPA installer for iOS. The demonstration URL must lead to the app store listing or a page providing the installation file.\n\nIf a desktop application supports only one operating system, its demonstration video must include a thorough visual walkthrough of the application's functionality. This helps judges understand the complete experience even if they cannot run the application on their own devices.\n\n### 2.3. Accessibility and Evaluation Requirements\n\nWe want judges and community members to be able to experience your work without unnecessary effort. A project should be straightforward to access and use, even for someone who has never seen it before.\n\nYou must not require judges to:\n\n- Create an account on an unrelated third-party platform.\n\n- Purchase hardware or pay for access to your project.\n\n- Install uncommon or unnecessary software just to evaluate it.\n\n- Complete unreasonable setup steps before seeing the main features.\n\nIf your project depends on specific hardware, peripherals, or a particular environment that judges may not have, you must provide a complete visual demonstration in your video. The video must still follow all audio and anonymity requirements described in Phase 3.\n\nA project that is difficult to access or evaluate may receive significant penalties, even if its underlying implementation is technically impressive.\n\n## Phase 3: Prepare and Submit Your Project\n\nBefore submitting, make sure that your repository, documentation, demonstration, and supporting materials are complete. The following requirements are mandatory unless explicitly marked otherwise.\n\n### 3.1. Public Source Code Repository\n\n**Required.** Your complete project source code must be hosted in a publicly accessible repository on a recognized version control platform. GitHub is the preferred platform because it matches the submission form and the organizers' evaluation workflow.\n\nAcceptable platforms include:\n\n- GitHub\n\n- GitLab\n\n- Codeberg\n\n- Gitee\n\nAny other platform is acceptable if it allows the public to browse and clone the complete repository without requiring authentication or payment.\n\nYour repository must contain the actual source code needed to understand and reproduce your project. **Providing only a compiled executable, a compressed archive, or another opaque form of delivery is not an acceptable substitute for a public source repository.**\n\nThe repository must remain publicly accessible for evaluation. Do not make it private, remove essential files, or restrict access while judging is in progress.\n\n### 3.2. README Documentation\n\n**Required.** Every repository must contain a file named `README.md` . It must be written in clear, professional American English and organized so that someone unfamiliar with your project can understand it.\n\nYour README must include all of the following:\n\n- **Project description:** A concise explanation of what your project does and what problem, idea, or purpose it addresses.\n\n- **Usage instructions:** Clear instructions explaining how to access and use the project. Include the live website link, download link, setup instructions, or a combination of these where appropriate.\n\n- **Technology list:** A list of the main programming languages, libraries, frameworks, and other technologies used.\n\n- **Demonstration video:** A direct link to the valid demonstration video described in Section 3.3.\n\nAny important limitations that affect evaluation, including account requirements or platform restrictions, must also be stated clearly.\n\n### 3.3. Demonstration Video\n\n**Required.** Every submission must include a demonstration video that clearly presents the main features and functionality of the project.\n\nThe video must meet **all** of the following requirements:\n\n**A. Duration**\n\nThe video must be between **two and three minutes long** . Videos outside this range do not satisfy the requirement.\n\n**B. No Audio or Personal Identification**\n\n**The video must contain absolutely no audio.** This includes narration, speech, music, sound effects, system audio, and other recorded sounds.\n\nThe video must not reveal the identity of any participant. This includes faces, names, voices, and other personal identifying information.\n\nAny violation of these anonymity requirements will result in immediate disqualification.\n\n**C. Video Encoding**\n\nThe video must use one of the following codecs:\n\n- H.264\n\n- AV1\n\n- VP9\n\nNo other codecs are permitted. Examples of unacceptable formats include HEVC and ProRes.\n\n**D. Approved Hosting Platforms**\n\nThe video must be hosted on one of these platforms:\n\n- YouTube\n\n- Bilibili\n\n- OneDrive or SharePoint\n\nYou must provide a publicly accessible, view-only sharing link. Anyone with the link must be able to watch the video without requesting permission or signing in.\n\nBefore submitting, open the link in an incognito or private browser window to confirm that it works.\n\n**A private, broken, or access-restricted link will be treated as a missing video and will result in disqualification.**\n\n**E. No Git-Based Video Hosting**\n\n**Uploading the demonstration video to GitHub or any other Git-based hosting platform is strictly forbidden.**\n\nA video hosted on a Git-based platform will be treated as if no valid video had been submitted, resulting in disqualification.\n\nThis rule helps protect shared development infrastructure. Large video files can consume unnecessary repository storage and make it harder for other developers to use the services they depend on. Choosing an appropriate video hosting service is a small but important part of responsible development.\n\n**F. Required SHA-256 Checksum**\n\nIf your project includes a downloadable executable, you must display the executable's **SHA-256 checksum** clearly in the demonstration video.\n\nThe checksum allows judges to verify that the executable they download is the same file shown in your demonstration.\n\nIf the checksum does not match and you cannot provide a valid explanation, the executable will be treated as potentially malicious. Confirmed malware will be handled under the rules in Section 5.3.\n\n**G. Required Walkthrough for Single-Platform Desktop Apps**\n\nIf your desktop application supports only one operating system, the demonstration video must include a thorough visual walkthrough of its functionality.\n\nThis walkthrough must make the application's main features and overall experience clear, even if a judge cannot run it directly.\n\n### 3.4. Intermediate Build Artifacts\n\n**Encouraged, but not required.** If your project is written in a language that compiles to native code or bytecode, we encourage you to include human-verifiable intermediate build artifacts in your repository.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- `.asm` assembly files\n\n- `.ll` LLVM Intermediate Representation files\n\n- `.class` Java bytecode files\n\n- Other comparable intermediate representations\n\nThese files can help judges understand how your source code is translated into executable instructions and provide another way to verify the relationship between your implementation and the demonstrated behavior.\n\nIncluding such artifacts is not mandatory, but doing so demonstrates an additional level of transparency and may be viewed positively during evaluation.\n\n### 3.5. Optional Extended Project Description\n\n**Optional.** In addition to the required README, you may submit a separate, more detailed written description of your project.\n\nThis document can explain your design decisions, implementation details, technical challenges, development process, and other features that may not be immediately clear from the demonstration video.\n\nA well-prepared extended description may strengthen your submission by helping judges understand the reasoning and work behind your project. It is not a substitute for any mandatory requirement.\n\n### 3.6. Final Submission Check\n\nBefore submitting, verify that:\n\n- Your source code repository is public and complete.\n\n- Your README contains all required information.\n\n- Your project can be accessed or downloaded through the demo URL.\n\n- Your demonstration video is between two and three minutes long.\n\n- The video contains no audio or personal identification.\n\n- The video uses an approved codec and hosting platform.\n\n- The video link works without signing in or requesting access.\n\n- The video is not hosted on a Git-based platform.\n\n- Your SHA-256 checksum is shown if you provide an executable.\n\n- Your AI usage disclosure is complete and accurate.\n\n- Your project complies with the originality and licensing requirements.\n\n## Phase 4: Judging, Verification, and Live Presentations\n\nEvery submission will undergo preliminary evaluation according to the competition judging rubric. In addition, the organizers may invite certain teams to participate in a live presentation and question-and-answer session.\n\nThis additional phase serves two purposes: verifying the integrity of submissions and confirming that prize candidates understand and can demonstrate their work.\n\n### 4.1. Preliminary Judging\n\nJudges will evaluate submissions according to the competition rubric. They may consider the project's functionality, implementation, originality, relevance to the theme, accessibility, documentation, and other criteria specified in the rubric.\n\nMeeting the minimum submission requirements does not guarantee a high score or an award. The quality and substance of the project remain important.\n\n### 4.2. Verification of AI Usage and Project Ownership\n\nIf a majority of the judges believe that a submission shows signs of excessive, undeclared AI use, the team will be asked to present the project and explain how it was developed.\n\nThe organizers may use AI-detection methods, now or in the future, as part of their review to identify possible excessive or undeclared AI use and work that appears to rely on unchecked AI-generated material.\n\nDuring the presentation, participants may be asked to explain their implementation, demonstrate important features, describe technical decisions, and answer questions about their source code.\n\n**Responsible, properly disclosed AI use is permitted and does not, by itself, trigger this verification process.**\n\nParticipation in a requested verification session is mandatory. Refusing to participate will result in automatic disqualification.\n\n### 4.3. Live Presentations for Prize Recipients\n\nAll projects selected for any of the following awards must complete a live presentation:\n\n- First Prize\n\n- Second Prize\n\n- Third Prize\n\n- Honorable Mention\n\nDuring this presentation, the team must demonstrate that it understands its project and can explain the work it has submitted.\n\nIf a prize candidate refuses to participate or cannot adequately demonstrate ownership and understanding of the project, the team will lose its prize eligibility and will instead be treated as a regular, non-prize submission.\n\nThis requirement applies to every prize category listed above, including Honorable Mention.\n\n### 4.4. Final Recognition\n\nThe competition awards First Prize, Second Prize, and Third Prize, each of which carries a guaranteed prize. Honorable Mentions may also be awarded, although they are not guaranteed to include a monetary award.\n\nThe exact monetary value of each prize is **tentative and subject to confirmation** . The organizers nevertheless intend to provide meaningful recognition to the selected projects.\n\n## Phase 5: Licensing, Public Showcase, and Project Rights\n\nSubmitting a project involves more than providing source code and a demonstration. Participants must also understand how their code may be licensed, how their projects may be presented publicly, and which rights they grant to the organizers.\n\n### 5.1. Source-Code License\n\n**An explicit open-source license is strongly encouraged.** Every participant should include a valid license file in the project repository, such as `LICENSE` or `LICENSE.md` .\n\nIf your repository contains a valid license file specifying an OSI-approved open-source license, that license will govern your project source code. Examples include:\n\n- MIT License\n\n- GNU General Public License (GPL)\n\n- Apache License 2.0\n\n- BSD licenses\n\n**Default MIT License provision:** If your repository does not contain an explicit, valid license file at the time of submission, you agree that your own project source code is automatically made available under the terms of the MIT License.\n\nThis provision is intended to ensure that the community can study, use, and build upon submitted work when no other open-source license has been specified. The rights granted by third-party libraries, assets, and other materials remain subject to their respective licenses.\n\n### 5.2. License to Showcase Submitted Projects\n\nBy submitting a project to nethack, you grant the organizers a **non-exclusive, worldwide, royalty-free, perpetual, irrevocable, and sublicensable license** to use, reproduce, modify, adapt, publish, translate, create derivative works from, distribute, and publicly display your submitted project, in whole or in part, through any medium.\n\nThis license is granted solely for the purpose of advertising, promoting, and showcasing the competition and its results.\n\nThis showcase license is separate from the source-code license you choose for your repository. It does not prevent you from continuing to develop, distribute, or license your own project under the terms you select.\n\nThe license also allows the organizers to authorize others to use the relevant material for the same promotional and showcase purposes.\n\n### 5.3. Malware, Deception, and Academic Integrity\n\nSoftware intended to harm, disrupt, damage, or gain unauthorized access to any system is strictly prohibited. Participants must not submit malware or use deceptive methods to gain an unfair advantage.\n\nExamples of prohibited conduct include, but are not limited to:\n\n- Submitting software designed to harm users or systems.\n\n- Attempting to access systems or data without authorization.\n\n- Providing source code that deliberately misrepresents the submitted executable.\n\n- Providing misleading or unexplained SHA-256 checksum information.\n\n- Making false claims about the use of AI tools or the originality of submitted work.\n\n- Deliberately misleading judges about the functionality or authorship of a project.\n\nConfirmed incidents will be treated as serious misconduct and academic dishonesty and will be reported to the upper school administration.\n\nSuch conduct is not simply a violation of competition rules. It also violates the trust and safety expected of everyone participating in this community.\n\n### 5.4. Public Listing of Participants and Projects\n\nBy entering the competition, you agree that your project may be included in the final public showcase of nethack.\n\nThe showcase may appear on the competition website, in promotional materials, or in press releases. It may include your project name, a brief project description, and other relevant presentation materials.\n\nParticipants who have legitimate privacy concerns must contact the organizers at least **24 hours before the submission deadline** . Requests will be considered by the organizers. If a participant requests that their project not be included in the public showcase and the request is accepted under the withdrawal procedure below, the submission will be disqualified.\n\n### 5.5. Withdrawal of Showcase Consent\n\nIf you do not wish to grant the organizers permission to showcase your project, you must contact the organizers with a legitimate reason at least **24 hours before the competition submission deadline** .\n\nIf the request is accepted, your submission will be disqualified from the competition.\n\nIf you attempt to withdraw showcase consent without meeting the stated deadline and requirements, the license granted in Section 5.2 will remain in full force and effect.\n\n### 5.6. Ownership and Permissions\n\nBy submitting your project, you represent and warrant that you are the owner of all rights necessary to submit the work and grant the permissions described in this document, or that you have obtained all necessary permissions and licenses from the relevant rights holders.\n\nYou also confirm that your submission does not violate the intellectual property rights of any third party or any applicable law.\n\n### 5.7. No Obligation to Use Submitted Work\n\nThe organizers are under no obligation to exercise any of the rights granted through the showcase license. Receiving permission to use submitted material does not require the organizers to publish, promote, distribute, or otherwise use it.\n\n### 5.8. Compensation\n\nParticipants agree that they will receive no compensation for the use of their projects under the showcase license beyond any prizes explicitly awarded by the competition.\n\n## Phase 6: General Terms and Responsibilities\n\n### 6.1. Financial Responsibility\n\nParticipants are responsible for all costs and expenses they incur while preparing for or taking part in the competition.\n\nThis includes, but is not limited to:\n\n- Fees for third-party software and online services.\n\n- Cloud computing, hosting, and server expenses.\n\n- AI tool subscriptions and usage fees.\n\n- Hardware, peripherals, and other equipment.\n\n- Any other development or participation expenses.\n\nThe organizers are not responsible for reimbursing these expenses. Any possible reimbursement of offline-event costs is **tentative and not guaranteed** . Participants should plan their budgets accordingly and should not assume that any expense will be covered.\n\n### 6.2. Limitation of Liability\n\nTo the extent permitted by applicable law, the organizers shall not be liable for any direct, indirect, incidental, special, or consequential damages arising from participation in the competition.\n\nThis includes, but is not limited to, loss of data, profits, business opportunities, or other losses resulting from participation, project development, submission, or the use of third-party services.\n\nThe organizers provide no express or implied warranties regarding the competition, judging process, or prizes. Participants submit their projects and take part in the competition at their own risk.\n\n### 6.3. Changes to the Competition and Rules\n\nThe organizers reserve the right to modify, suspend, or cancel the competition at any time for any reason. They may also amend these rules when necessary and will provide reasonable notice of changes.\n\nThe organizers are responsible for interpreting and applying these rules. Their decisions regarding the competition and the interpretation of its requirements are **final and binding** .\n\nIf any provision of these rules is found to be invalid or unenforceable, the remaining provisions will continue to apply to the fullest extent permitted by applicable law.\n\n### 6.4. Contact and Clarification\n\nIf you have questions about these rules, need clarification on a requirement, or wish to raise a concern regarding the competition, please contact the organizers through email or Microsoft Teams:\n\n[devclub-bisz@basischina.com](mailto:devclub-bisz@basischina.com)\n\nMembers of the BISZ Developers' Club leadership will respond to inquiries.\n\n### 6.5. Time Zone and Deadlines\n\nEvery deadline published on nethack uses **Asia/Shanghai (UTC+8)** . Timestamps on the website show this zone explicitly. Participants in other zones are responsible for converting the deadline correctly; the platform closes submissions at the displayed time, not at a participant's local time.\n\n### 6.6. Late Submissions\n\nSubmissions received after the published deadline are not accepted. The only exception is a documented platform failure that prevented submission before the deadline and that the organizers can verify from server logs. A local computer, network, or account problem is not grounds for an extension. Contact the organizers immediately if you believe a platform failure affected your submission.\n\n### 6.7. Clarifications, Appeals, and Arbitration\n\nQuestions about the rules should be sent before the submission deadline so the answer can be applied consistently. If a participant believes a judging result, penalty, or eligibility decision is wrong, they may appeal in writing to [devclub-bisz@basischina.com](mailto:devclub-bisz@basischina.com) within 48 hours of the published result. The appeal must identify the team, the decision, and the evidence the participant wants reviewed.\n\nThe BIBS-C Developers' Club leadership reviews appeals as a panel and may request a live explanation or additional evidence. Panel decisions are final and binding. Appeals that are abusive, repetitive without new evidence, or filed after the 48-hour window may be declined without review.\n\n### 6.8. Privacy and Data Retention\n\nThe organizers process basis-auth account identifiers, names, email addresses, team membership, project submissions, peer ballots, judge evaluations, and administrative audit records to run the competition and publish the showcase. Access is limited to organizers and judges who need the data for their role. Passwords, authentication tokens, and private credentials are never displayed to other participants.\n\nCompetition records are retained for the life of the nethack archive so results and past seasons remain verifiable. Anonymous judge comments may remain visible to a team after publication. Participants may request access, correction, or deletion of their personal data by writing to the contact address above; deletion requests that would remove a published result or violate an archival obligation will be handled by explaining the remaining legal or competition basis. Public showcase consent and its withdrawal deadline are described in Phase 5.\n\nMay careful work take root and grow, May curiosity guide what you know. May shared ideas help good work shine, And leave something useful for all in time.\n";

export const RULES_BODY_MARKDOWN =
    "This document sets out the rules, expectations, and terms governing participation in nethack. It explains what participants must provide, how projects will be evaluated, and the standards of conduct expected throughout the competition.\n\n**Version 1.0.** This version took effect on 21 November 2026 and is the binding document for the current season. Organizers may publish a later version with reasonable notice; the latest published version governs participation and submissions. Participants are responsible for reviewing it before submitting their work.\n\n**Please read before participating.**\n\nThe requirements in this document are binding. A project that works on its creator's computer is not necessarily ready for evaluation. Participants must also meet the source code, documentation, accessibility, demonstration, and conduct requirements described below.\n\n### Quick Guide: What You Need to Know Recommended for readers who prefer a simpler, plain-language explanation.\n\nThis section explains the most important rules in plain language. It is a starting point, not a replacement for reading the complete requirements below.\n\n**1. Make Your Source Code Public**\n\nYour complete project source code must be available in a public repository on a platform such as GitHub, GitLab, Codeberg, or Gitee. **Uploading only an executable file or a compressed archive is not enough.** Other people must be able to browse and download your source code.\n\n**2. Include a Complete README**\n\nYour repository must contain a file named `README.md` , written in clear, professional American English. It must explain what your project does, how to use it, which technologies you used, and where to watch your demonstration video.\n\n**3. Make Your Project Available for Testing**\n\nJudges must have a practical way to try your project. Depending on what you build:\n\n- Websites must be deployed and publicly accessible.\n\n- Desktop applications must have downloadable executables for at least one supported platform.\n\n- Games must be playable in a browser or available as a download.\n\n- Mobile applications must be available through an app store or as an APK or IPA installer.\n\nJudges should not have to create an account on another website just to see your work. If your own application requires user accounts, additional requirements and penalties may apply.\n\n**4. Follow the Demonstration Video Rules**\n\nEvery project must have a demonstration video **between two and three minutes long** . The video must contain **absolutely no audio** and must not reveal your identity, including your face, name, or voice.\n\nThe video must use H.264, AV1, or VP9 encoding and must be hosted on YouTube, Bilibili, or OneDrive/SharePoint through an accessible sharing link.\n\n**Do not upload the video to GitHub or any other Git-based hosting platform.** Doing so will result in the submission being treated as having no valid video and will lead to disqualification.\n\nIf your project includes a downloadable executable, you must also display its SHA-256 checksum in the video.\n\n**5. Make Evaluation Easy**\n\nJudges should be able to experience your project without purchasing hardware, installing uncommon software, or signing up for unrelated services. Any unavoidable access requirements must be explained in your README and may affect your evaluation.\n\n**6. Submit Your Own Work**\n\nYour project must represent meaningful original work. You may use existing libraries, frameworks, and assets when you have the right to do so and provide proper credit. Simply copying an existing project without meaningful changes is plagiarism and will result in disqualification.\n\n**7. Be Honest About AI**\n\nAI tools are allowed, but you must explain exactly which parts of your project they helped create, which tools you used, and what assistance they provided. A vague statement such as \"AI was used\" is not enough. Undeclared or misleading AI use may be treated as plagiarism.\n\n**8. Be Prepared to Explain Your Work**\n\nJudges may ask you to present your project and answer questions if they suspect excessive or undeclared AI use. All First Prize, Second Prize, Third Prize, and Honorable Mention recipients must also complete a live presentation.\n\n**9. Respect Others and Their Work**\n\nHarmful software, unauthorized access, deliberate deception, harassment, discrimination, and plagiarism are prohibited. Serious violations may be reported to the school administration.\n\n**10. Understand the Licensing Terms**\n\nBy submitting a project, you agree to the source-code licensing and public showcase terms described in this document. If you do not provide an explicit license file, the default MIT licensing provision applies as described below.\n\n**11. Understand Your Responsibilities**\n\nParticipants are responsible for their own expenses. Prize amounts are subject to confirmation, and the organizers reserve the right to amend these rules or make changes to the competition when necessary.\n\n**12. Ask Questions When Necessary**\n\nFor questions or clarification, contact **devclub-bisz@basischina.com** . Members of the BISZ Developers' Club leadership will respond.\n\n## Essential Requirements at a Glance\n\nThe following checklist summarizes the main submission requirements. Read the detailed sections for the exact rules and consequences.\n\n- **Public source code:** Complete source code must be publicly available on a recognized Git hosting platform. Executable-only submissions are not accepted.\n\n- **README:** A complete, professional `README.md` written in American English is required.\n\n- **Working demonstration:** A publicly accessible demo URL and a valid demonstration video are required.\n\n- **Evaluation access:** Judges must not be forced to create unrelated third-party accounts, purchase hardware, or install uncommon software.\n\n- **Original work:** Submissions must contain meaningful original contributions. Closed-source projects are not eligible.\n\n- **AI disclosure:** All substantial AI-generated or AI-assisted project components must be described in detail.\n\n- **Video requirements:** The video must be 2–3 minutes long, silent, and free of personal identification. It must not be hosted on a Git-based platform.\n\n- **Costs:** Participants are responsible for expenses incurred during development and participation.\n\n- **Showcase permission:** Submission grants the organizers the rights described in the public showcase license section.\n\n- **Default source-code license:** If no explicit license file is provided, the MIT License provision described below applies.\n\n**Important: Mandatory requirements**\n\nRequirements explicitly marked as mandatory must be met. Failure to meet a mandatory submission requirement will result in disqualification unless that particular rule expressly specifies a different consequence, such as a scoring penalty.\n\n## Phase 1: Plan Your Project and Understand the Rules\n\nBefore beginning development, make sure that your project fits the competition, that you have the right to use everything you include, and that you understand the responsibilities that come with submitting your work.\n\n### 1.1. Thematic Relevance\n\nEvery submission must have a meaningful connection to the competition theme. A minimal connection is required, and projects with a particularly weak or superficial connection will receive point deductions according to the judging rubric.\n\nYour project should demonstrate that you have thought about the theme and applied it to something meaningful. Simply mentioning the theme in a project description is not enough to establish a strong connection.\n\n### 1.2. Originality and Intellectual Property\n\nAll submissions must represent your team's own original work. You are welcome to build on existing technologies, including open-source libraries, frameworks, and other resources, provided that you have the legal right to use them and give appropriate credit to their creators.\n\nYou must respect all applicable licenses and intellectual property rights. Do not include copyrighted material, source code, images, music, or other assets without permission or a license that allows your intended use.\n\n**Copying or directly forking an existing project without making meaningful original contributions constitutes plagiarism.** Such submissions will be disqualified and may be reported to the school administration.\n\nUsing an existing library or framework does not, by itself, make a project unoriginal. What matters is whether your team has made meaningful contributions of its own and can explain the work it submits.\n\n### 1.3. Open-Source Development\n\nnethack is built around the belief that software development benefits from openness, transparency, and collaboration. We want participants to create work that others can study, learn from, and improve.\n\n**Closed-source submissions are not eligible.** Every project must make its complete source code publicly available under an appropriate open-source license.\n\nWe do not dismiss the value of closed-source software. Rather, this competition specifically chooses to support open development because it encourages learning, makes collaboration possible, and helps build trust within the wider software community.\n\n### 1.4. Responsible Use of Artificial Intelligence\n\n**Required disclosure.** AI tools are permitted in this competition. We recognize that they can help participants learn, explore ideas, solve problems, and develop software more efficiently. However, using AI does not remove your responsibility for the work you submit.\n\nEvery project component that was generated or substantially assisted by an AI tool must be disclosed. Your disclosure must identify:\n\n- **The specific parts involved:** Identify the components or files that received AI assistance, such as user interface components, backend logic, database structures, or scripts.\n\n- **The tools used:** Name the AI tools or models involved, such as ChatGPT, GitHub Copilot, or a locally run model.\n\n- **The type of assistance:** Explain what the tool actually did. For example, you might state that an AI tool generated a data-processing script that your team subsequently reviewed, tested, and modified.\n\nA general statement such as \"AI was used during development\" is not sufficient. The disclosure must allow judges to understand where AI contributed and how your team used its output.\n\n**Responsible and fully disclosed AI use is not considered plagiarism.** However, hiding substantial AI assistance, making misleading claims about your work, or relying on generated material that your team does not understand may result in the submission being treated as plagiarism.\n\nWe encourage participants to treat AI as a learning and development tool, not as a substitute for understanding. Being able to explain, review, test, and improve AI-assisted work is an important part of responsible software development.\n\n### 1.5. Participant Conduct and Community Standards\n\nAll participants are expected to act responsibly and treat one another with respect. Harassment, discrimination, intimidation, and other forms of harmful conduct will not be tolerated.\n\nThe organizers will assess the seriousness of reported incidents. Serious or repeated violations may be referred to the school administration for further action.\n\nParticipants are also expected to respect the work and contributions of others. Do not claim someone else's work as your own, deliberately misrepresent your contributions, or interfere with another participant's ability to take part in the competition.\n\n### 1.6. Principles of Open Participation\n\nBy participating in nethack, you agree to uphold the following principles. They form the ethical foundation of this competition and the community we hope to build.\n\n- Build with the intention of creating useful work and providing real value, not causing harm or disruption.\n\n- Share your work openly and contribute to the wider community's ability to learn and create.\n\n- Respect the same freedoms that you benefit from. Do not unnecessarily restrict others' access to knowledge, tools, or opportunities.\n\n- Act with integrity. Take responsibility for your work and respect the contributions and rights of others.\n\n## Phase 2: Build a Project That Can Be Evaluated\n\nA successful submission must be more than a collection of source files. Judges must have a practical way to access, understand, and test your project. Plan for this from the beginning rather than leaving it until the final day.\n\n### 2.1. Public Access and Demonstration\n\nEvery project must provide a publicly accessible demonstration URL. The link must lead directly to the working project, a playable version, or a page where the project can be downloaded.\n\nThe project must be available to judges without unnecessary restrictions. In particular, judges must not be required to create an account on an unrelated third-party website simply to access or experience your submission.\n\nIf authentication is an essential part of your own application, your system must allow any new user to register freely. Providing only one shared demonstration account is not acceptable. Even when registration is available, requiring judges to create an account introduces an evaluation barrier and may result in significant penalties.\n\nIf your project is hosted on a platform that inherently requires its users to sign in, you must clearly disclose this limitation in your README. You should expect such a requirement to negatively affect your evaluation.\n\n### 2.2. Requirements by Project Type\n\nThe exact form of your demonstration depends on the type of project you build. The following requirements apply:\n\n- **Websites and web applications:** Your project must be deployed on a publicly accessible server. Judges must be able to visit and use the live application through a browser. If your application requires authentication, it must allow new users to register freely. A single shared demonstration account is not sufficient.\n\n- **Desktop applications:** Your project must be compiled into a downloadable executable for at least one of the following: Windows, macOS, or Debian/Debian-based Linux. Supporting at least two of these platforms is **strongly encouraged** . The download link must lead to a page where the executable can be obtained, such as a repository release.\n\n- **Games:** Your game must be playable directly in a web browser or available as a downloadable executable. Platforms such as itch.io may be used to host browser-playable games or downloads.\n\n- **Mobile applications:** Your application must be published in the appropriate app store or made readily available through an APK installer for Android or an IPA installer for iOS. The demonstration URL must lead to the app store listing or a page providing the installation file.\n\nIf a desktop application supports only one operating system, its demonstration video must include a thorough visual walkthrough of the application's functionality. This helps judges understand the complete experience even if they cannot run the application on their own devices.\n\n### 2.3. Accessibility and Evaluation Requirements\n\nWe want judges and community members to be able to experience your work without unnecessary effort. A project should be straightforward to access and use, even for someone who has never seen it before.\n\nYou must not require judges to:\n\n- Create an account on an unrelated third-party platform.\n\n- Purchase hardware or pay for access to your project.\n\n- Install uncommon or unnecessary software just to evaluate it.\n\n- Complete unreasonable setup steps before seeing the main features.\n\nIf your project depends on specific hardware, peripherals, or a particular environment that judges may not have, you must provide a complete visual demonstration in your video. The video must still follow all audio and anonymity requirements described in Phase 3.\n\nA project that is difficult to access or evaluate may receive significant penalties, even if its underlying implementation is technically impressive.\n\n## Phase 3: Prepare and Submit Your Project\n\nBefore submitting, make sure that your repository, documentation, demonstration, and supporting materials are complete. The following requirements are mandatory unless explicitly marked otherwise.\n\n### 3.1. Public Source Code Repository\n\n**Required.** Your complete project source code must be hosted in a publicly accessible repository on a recognized version control platform. GitHub is the preferred platform because it matches the submission form and the organizers' evaluation workflow.\n\nAcceptable platforms include:\n\n- GitHub\n\n- GitLab\n\n- Codeberg\n\n- Gitee\n\nAny other platform is acceptable if it allows the public to browse and clone the complete repository without requiring authentication or payment.\n\nYour repository must contain the actual source code needed to understand and reproduce your project. **Providing only a compiled executable, a compressed archive, or another opaque form of delivery is not an acceptable substitute for a public source repository.**\n\nThe repository must remain publicly accessible for evaluation. Do not make it private, remove essential files, or restrict access while judging is in progress.\n\n### 3.2. README Documentation\n\n**Required.** Every repository must contain a file named `README.md` . It must be written in clear, professional American English and organized so that someone unfamiliar with your project can understand it.\n\nYour README must include all of the following:\n\n- **Project description:** A concise explanation of what your project does and what problem, idea, or purpose it addresses.\n\n- **Usage instructions:** Clear instructions explaining how to access and use the project. Include the live website link, download link, setup instructions, or a combination of these where appropriate.\n\n- **Technology list:** A list of the main programming languages, libraries, frameworks, and other technologies used.\n\n- **Demonstration video:** A direct link to the valid demonstration video described in Section 3.3.\n\nAny important limitations that affect evaluation, including account requirements or platform restrictions, must also be stated clearly.\n\n### 3.3. Demonstration Video\n\n**Required.** Every submission must include a demonstration video that clearly presents the main features and functionality of the project.\n\nThe video must meet **all** of the following requirements:\n\n**A. Duration**\n\nThe video must be between **two and three minutes long** . Videos outside this range do not satisfy the requirement.\n\n**B. No Audio or Personal Identification**\n\n**The video must contain absolutely no audio.** This includes narration, speech, music, sound effects, system audio, and other recorded sounds.\n\nThe video must not reveal the identity of any participant. This includes faces, names, voices, and other personal identifying information.\n\nAny violation of these anonymity requirements will result in immediate disqualification.\n\n**C. Video Encoding**\n\nThe video must use one of the following codecs:\n\n- H.264\n\n- AV1\n\n- VP9\n\nNo other codecs are permitted. Examples of unacceptable formats include HEVC and ProRes.\n\n**D. Approved Hosting Platforms**\n\nThe video must be hosted on one of these platforms:\n\n- YouTube\n\n- Bilibili\n\n- OneDrive or SharePoint\n\nYou must provide a publicly accessible, view-only sharing link. Anyone with the link must be able to watch the video without requesting permission or signing in.\n\nBefore submitting, open the link in an incognito or private browser window to confirm that it works.\n\n**A private, broken, or access-restricted link will be treated as a missing video and will result in disqualification.**\n\n**E. No Git-Based Video Hosting**\n\n**Uploading the demonstration video to GitHub or any other Git-based hosting platform is strictly forbidden.**\n\nA video hosted on a Git-based platform will be treated as if no valid video had been submitted, resulting in disqualification.\n\nThis rule helps protect shared development infrastructure. Large video files can consume unnecessary repository storage and make it harder for other developers to use the services they depend on. Choosing an appropriate video hosting service is a small but important part of responsible development.\n\n**F. Required SHA-256 Checksum**\n\nIf your project includes a downloadable executable, you must display the executable's **SHA-256 checksum** clearly in the demonstration video.\n\nThe checksum allows judges to verify that the executable they download is the same file shown in your demonstration.\n\nIf the checksum does not match and you cannot provide a valid explanation, the executable will be treated as potentially malicious. Confirmed malware will be handled under the rules in Section 5.3.\n\n**G. Required Walkthrough for Single-Platform Desktop Apps**\n\nIf your desktop application supports only one operating system, the demonstration video must include a thorough visual walkthrough of its functionality.\n\nThis walkthrough must make the application's main features and overall experience clear, even if a judge cannot run it directly.\n\n### 3.4. Intermediate Build Artifacts\n\n**Encouraged, but not required.** If your project is written in a language that compiles to native code or bytecode, we encourage you to include human-verifiable intermediate build artifacts in your repository.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- `.asm` assembly files\n\n- `.ll` LLVM Intermediate Representation files\n\n- `.class` Java bytecode files\n\n- Other comparable intermediate representations\n\nThese files can help judges understand how your source code is translated into executable instructions and provide another way to verify the relationship between your implementation and the demonstrated behavior.\n\nIncluding such artifacts is not mandatory, but doing so demonstrates an additional level of transparency and may be viewed positively during evaluation.\n\n### 3.5. Optional Extended Project Description\n\n**Optional.** In addition to the required README, you may submit a separate, more detailed written description of your project.\n\nThis document can explain your design decisions, implementation details, technical challenges, development process, and other features that may not be immediately clear from the demonstration video.\n\nA well-prepared extended description may strengthen your submission by helping judges understand the reasoning and work behind your project. It is not a substitute for any mandatory requirement.\n\n### 3.6. Final Submission Check\n\nBefore submitting, verify that:\n\n- Your source code repository is public and complete.\n\n- Your README contains all required information.\n\n- Your project can be accessed or downloaded through the demo URL.\n\n- Your demonstration video is between two and three minutes long.\n\n- The video contains no audio or personal identification.\n\n- The video uses an approved codec and hosting platform.\n\n- The video link works without signing in or requesting access.\n\n- The video is not hosted on a Git-based platform.\n\n- Your SHA-256 checksum is shown if you provide an executable.\n\n- Your AI usage disclosure is complete and accurate.\n\n- Your project complies with the originality and licensing requirements.\n\n## Phase 4: Judging, Verification, and Live Presentations\n\nEvery submission will undergo preliminary evaluation according to the competition judging rubric. In addition, the organizers may invite certain teams to participate in a live presentation and question-and-answer session.\n\nThis additional phase serves two purposes: verifying the integrity of submissions and confirming that prize candidates understand and can demonstrate their work.\n\n### 4.1. Preliminary Judging\n\nJudges will evaluate submissions according to the competition rubric. They may consider the project's functionality, implementation, originality, relevance to the theme, accessibility, documentation, and other criteria specified in the rubric.\n\nMeeting the minimum submission requirements does not guarantee a high score or an award. The quality and substance of the project remain important.\n\n### 4.2. Verification of AI Usage and Project Ownership\n\nIf a majority of the judges believe that a submission shows signs of excessive, undeclared AI use, the team will be asked to present the project and explain how it was developed.\n\nThe organizers may use AI-detection methods, now or in the future, as part of their review to identify possible excessive or undeclared AI use and work that appears to rely on unchecked AI-generated material.\n\nDuring the presentation, participants may be asked to explain their implementation, demonstrate important features, describe technical decisions, and answer questions about their source code.\n\n**Responsible, properly disclosed AI use is permitted and does not, by itself, trigger this verification process.**\n\nParticipation in a requested verification session is mandatory. Refusing to participate will result in automatic disqualification.\n\n### 4.3. Live Presentations for Prize Recipients\n\nAll projects selected for any of the following awards must complete a live presentation:\n\n- First Prize\n\n- Second Prize\n\n- Third Prize\n\n- Honorable Mention\n\nDuring this presentation, the team must demonstrate that it understands its project and can explain the work it has submitted.\n\nIf a prize candidate refuses to participate or cannot adequately demonstrate ownership and understanding of the project, the team will lose its prize eligibility and will instead be treated as a regular, non-prize submission.\n\nThis requirement applies to every prize category listed above, including Honorable Mention.\n\n### 4.4. Final Recognition\n\nThe competition awards First Prize, Second Prize, and Third Prize, each of which carries a guaranteed prize. Honorable Mentions may also be awarded, although they are not guaranteed to include a monetary award.\n\nThe exact monetary value of each prize is **tentative and subject to confirmation** . The organizers nevertheless intend to provide meaningful recognition to the selected projects.\n\n## Phase 5: Licensing, Public Showcase, and Project Rights\n\nSubmitting a project involves more than providing source code and a demonstration. Participants must also understand how their code may be licensed, how their projects may be presented publicly, and which rights they grant to the organizers.\n\n### 5.1. Source-Code License\n\n**An explicit open-source license is strongly encouraged.** Every participant should include a valid license file in the project repository, such as `LICENSE` or `LICENSE.md` .\n\nIf your repository contains a valid license file specifying an OSI-approved open-source license, that license will govern your project source code. Examples include:\n\n- MIT License\n\n- GNU General Public License (GPL)\n\n- Apache License 2.0\n\n- BSD licenses\n\n**Default MIT License provision:** If your repository does not contain an explicit, valid license file at the time of submission, you agree that your own project source code is automatically made available under the terms of the MIT License.\n\nThis provision is intended to ensure that the community can study, use, and build upon submitted work when no other open-source license has been specified. The rights granted by third-party libraries, assets, and other materials remain subject to their respective licenses.\n\n### 5.2. License to Showcase Submitted Projects\n\nBy submitting a project to nethack, you grant the organizers a **non-exclusive, worldwide, royalty-free, perpetual, irrevocable, and sublicensable license** to use, reproduce, modify, adapt, publish, translate, create derivative works from, distribute, and publicly display your submitted project, in whole or in part, through any medium.\n\nThis license is granted solely for the purpose of advertising, promoting, and showcasing the competition and its results.\n\nThis showcase license is separate from the source-code license you choose for your repository. It does not prevent you from continuing to develop, distribute, or license your own project under the terms you select.\n\nThe license also allows the organizers to authorize others to use the relevant material for the same promotional and showcase purposes.\n\n### 5.3. Malware, Deception, and Academic Integrity\n\nSoftware intended to harm, disrupt, damage, or gain unauthorized access to any system is strictly prohibited. Participants must not submit malware or use deceptive methods to gain an unfair advantage.\n\nExamples of prohibited conduct include, but are not limited to:\n\n- Submitting software designed to harm users or systems.\n\n- Attempting to access systems or data without authorization.\n\n- Providing source code that deliberately misrepresents the submitted executable.\n\n- Providing misleading or unexplained SHA-256 checksum information.\n\n- Making false claims about the use of AI tools or the originality of submitted work.\n\n- Deliberately misleading judges about the functionality or authorship of a project.\n\nConfirmed incidents will be treated as serious misconduct and academic dishonesty and will be reported to the upper school administration.\n\nSuch conduct is not simply a violation of competition rules. It also violates the trust and safety expected of everyone participating in this community.\n\n### 5.4. Public Listing of Participants and Projects\n\nBy entering the competition, you agree that your project may be included in the final public showcase of nethack.\n\nThe showcase may appear on the competition website, in promotional materials, or in press releases. It may include your project name, a brief project description, and other relevant presentation materials.\n\nParticipants who have legitimate privacy concerns must contact the organizers at least **24 hours before the submission deadline** . Requests will be considered by the organizers. If a participant requests that their project not be included in the public showcase and the request is accepted under the withdrawal procedure below, the submission will be disqualified.\n\n### 5.5. Withdrawal of Showcase Consent\n\nIf you do not wish to grant the organizers permission to showcase your project, you must contact the organizers with a legitimate reason at least **24 hours before the competition submission deadline** .\n\nIf the request is accepted, your submission will be disqualified from the competition.\n\nIf you attempt to withdraw showcase consent without meeting the stated deadline and requirements, the license granted in Section 5.2 will remain in full force and effect.\n\n### 5.6. Ownership and Permissions\n\nBy submitting your project, you represent and warrant that you are the owner of all rights necessary to submit the work and grant the permissions described in this document, or that you have obtained all necessary permissions and licenses from the relevant rights holders.\n\nYou also confirm that your submission does not violate the intellectual property rights of any third party or any applicable law.\n\n### 5.7. No Obligation to Use Submitted Work\n\nThe organizers are under no obligation to exercise any of the rights granted through the showcase license. Receiving permission to use submitted material does not require the organizers to publish, promote, distribute, or otherwise use it.\n\n### 5.8. Compensation\n\nParticipants agree that they will receive no compensation for the use of their projects under the showcase license beyond any prizes explicitly awarded by the competition.\n\n## Phase 6: General Terms and Responsibilities\n\n### 6.1. Financial Responsibility\n\nParticipants are responsible for all costs and expenses they incur while preparing for or taking part in the competition.\n\nThis includes, but is not limited to:\n\n- Fees for third-party software and online services.\n\n- Cloud computing, hosting, and server expenses.\n\n- AI tool subscriptions and usage fees.\n\n- Hardware, peripherals, and other equipment.\n\n- Any other development or participation expenses.\n\nThe organizers are not responsible for reimbursing these expenses. Any possible reimbursement of offline-event costs is **tentative and not guaranteed** . Participants should plan their budgets accordingly and should not assume that any expense will be covered.\n\n### 6.2. Limitation of Liability\n\nTo the extent permitted by applicable law, the organizers shall not be liable for any direct, indirect, incidental, special, or consequential damages arising from participation in the competition.\n\nThis includes, but is not limited to, loss of data, profits, business opportunities, or other losses resulting from participation, project development, submission, or the use of third-party services.\n\nThe organizers provide no express or implied warranties regarding the competition, judging process, or prizes. Participants submit their projects and take part in the competition at their own risk.\n\n### 6.3. Changes to the Competition and Rules\n\nThe organizers reserve the right to modify, suspend, or cancel the competition at any time for any reason. They may also amend these rules when necessary and will provide reasonable notice of changes.\n\nThe organizers are responsible for interpreting and applying these rules. Their decisions regarding the competition and the interpretation of its requirements are **final and binding** .\n\nIf any provision of these rules is found to be invalid or unenforceable, the remaining provisions will continue to apply to the fullest extent permitted by applicable law.\n\n### 6.4. Contact and Clarification\n\nIf you have questions about these rules, need clarification on a requirement, or wish to raise a concern regarding the competition, please contact the organizers through email or Microsoft Teams:\n\n[devclub-bisz@basischina.com](mailto:devclub-bisz@basischina.com)\n\nMembers of the BISZ Developers' Club leadership will respond to inquiries.\n\n### 6.5. Time Zone and Deadlines\n\nEvery deadline published on nethack uses **Asia/Shanghai (UTC+8)** . Timestamps on the website show this zone explicitly. Participants in other zones are responsible for converting the deadline correctly; the platform closes submissions at the displayed time, not at a participant's local time.\n\n### 6.6. Late Submissions\n\nSubmissions received after the published deadline are not accepted. The only exception is a documented platform failure that prevented submission before the deadline and that the organizers can verify from server logs. A local computer, network, or account problem is not grounds for an extension. Contact the organizers immediately if you believe a platform failure affected your submission.\n\n### 6.7. Clarifications, Appeals, and Arbitration\n\nQuestions about the rules should be sent before the submission deadline so the answer can be applied consistently. If a participant believes a judging result, penalty, or eligibility decision is wrong, they may appeal in writing to [devclub-bisz@basischina.com](mailto:devclub-bisz@basischina.com) within 48 hours of the published result. The appeal must identify the team, the decision, and the evidence the participant wants reviewed.\n\nThe BIBS-C Developers' Club leadership reviews appeals as a panel and may request a live explanation or additional evidence. Panel decisions are final and binding. Appeals that are abusive, repetitive without new evidence, or filed after the 48-hour window may be declined without review.\n\n### 6.8. Privacy and Data Retention\n\nThe organizers process basis-auth account identifiers, names, email addresses, team membership, project submissions, peer ballots, judge evaluations, and administrative audit records to run the competition and publish the showcase. Access is limited to organizers and judges who need the data for their role. Passwords, authentication tokens, and private credentials are never displayed to other participants.\n\nCompetition records are retained for the life of the nethack archive so results and past seasons remain verifiable. Anonymous judge comments may remain visible to a team after publication. Participants may request access, correction, or deletion of their personal data by writing to the contact address above; deletion requests that would remove a published result or violate an archival obligation will be handled by explaining the remaining legal or competition basis. Public showcase consent and its withdrawal deadline are described in Phase 5.\n\nMay careful work take root and grow, May curiosity guide what you know. May shared ideas help good work shine, And leave something useful for all in time.\n";
